{"step_index":303,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-21T21:52:25Z","thinking":"**Considering the Scope**\n\nI'm currently focused on defining the parameters of this project. The user's requirement for a VBA macro solution within the Excel file itself is clear. I'm noting the acceptance of the macro-enabled `.xlsm` file type. Now I am working to understand exactly what needs to go into the \"Needs Review\" column.\n\n\n**Defining Macro Implementation**\n\nNow I'm delving into the core functionality: row movement based on dropdown selection. If \"✅ مطابق\" is chosen, the row heads to the specified warehouse sheet; \"❌ غير مطابق\" redirects to the \"لم يُعثر عليه\" sheet. I need to make sure this transfers over any values. I need to think through the complexities with error handling.\n\n\n**Exploring Macro Automation**\n\nI've determined that direct Python-to-VBA generation isn't feasible, but there are workarounds. I'm prioritizing programmatic approaches. My current focus is on a template-based macro method. This entails creating a `.xlsm` template with the VBA code, which Python then populates with data. It looks like the user is asking for the VBA solution to be implemented and added, rather than the VBA code to be provided separately. I'm leaning toward this template strategy for a seamless solution.\n\n\n**Refining Automation Approach**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on a robust and reliable automation strategy. The initial idea of generating `.xlsm` directly has evolved. I've considered using `xlsxwriter` but realized that obtaining the `vbaProject.bin` file isn't straightforward. Instead, I'm leaning toward a combined approach. First I will write the VBA code in a text file. Then, I'm investigating a Python script that uses `win32com.client` to invisibly inject the code into the Excel workbook and save it. It will be implemented as a VBA solution. I'll write out the exact VBA code now!\n\n\n**Constructing Core VBA Code**\n\n