Trust access to VBA project\" security setting, so I may need to create a review GUI. I considered `xlsxwriter` and `vbaProject.bin`, but that doesn't seem to work, either. The main goal here is avoiding manual steps to reach 100% automation.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"python -c \\\"import win32com.client; excel = win32com.client.Dispatch('Excel.Application'); excel.Quit(); print('OK')\\\"\"","Cwd":"\"d:\\\\AI_Engineer\\\\Pharmacy-agy\"","IsDaemon":"false","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"2000","toolAction":"\"Testing win32com availability\"","toolSummary":"\"Test win32com\""}}]}
{"step_index":309,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-21T21:56:09Z","thinking":"**Fixing the Command Tool**\n\nI'm frustrated. The `run_command` tool is still completely unusable. It's failing because the PowerShell executable isn't being found in the %PATH% environment variable, so I cannot execute any terminal commands, thus I cannot generate the file.\n\n\n**Exploring Automation Options**\n\nI've been exploring a few ways to automate this, and I'm currently leaning towards integrating VBA code directly. My initial idea of a Python script injecting the macro using `win32com` has a major flaw: users need to enable a specific Excel setting which could cause this whole system to crash. I'm also considering creating a `base64` string of a pre-filled template and injecting this data into the Excel file. This avoids the requirement for external configuration.\n\n\n**Proposing Python Workflow**\n\nI've hit on a promising solution! Abandoning VBA for now, I'm proposing a two-step Python workflow. First, `fast_main.py` generates `Review_Phase.xlsx` with dropdowns. Then, after the pharmacist reviews and saves, a batch script (or `finalize.py`) reads their choices, moves data, and outputs `Final_Order.xlsx`. This eliminates VBA, macros, security concerns, and manual setup – a much safer and fully automated route!\n\n\n**Refining Script Architecture**\n\nI'm now fully committed to a completely automated, two-step Python process, avoiding VBA altogether. I realize this is more robust and easier than my previous approaches. The pharmacist will interact only with the Excel file and make their selections. Then, a script will process the user selections, move data to its appropriate location, and produce the final, print-ready document. This gets rid of all manual coding.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"CodeContent":"\"import sys\\nfrom pathlib import Path\\nfrom openpyxl import load_workbook\\nimport shutil\\n\\n#